</p>
<h3>Perimeter calculations</h3>
<h4><code>&lt;line&gt;</code></h4>
<p>Just the Euclidean distance based on <code>x1</code>, <code>y1</code>, <code>x2</code>, <code>y2</code></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function getEuclideanDistance(x1, y1, x2, y2) {
    let dx = x2 - x1;
    let dy = y2 - y1;
    // horizontal or vertical
    if(!dx || !dy) return Math.abs(dx+dy)
    return Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);
}
</code></pre>
<h4><code>&lt;polyline&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;polygon&gt;</code></h4>
<p>The vertices are specified in the <code>points</code> attribute. It doesn't matter if values are separated by commas or white-space – this also includes new lines or tabs.<br />
As mentioned earlier: polys can only use absolute coordinates – no need for normalization!<br />
We only have to run through the coordinate array in point pairs – or in this case using the flat array in steps of 4 values</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function getPolygonLength(coords=[], isPoly=false){
    let len = 0;
    let l=coords.length;
    let x1, y1, x2, y2;

    for(let i=3; i&lt;l; i+=2){
        [x1, y1, x2, y2] = [coords[i-3], coords[i-2], coords[i-1], coords[i]]
        len += getEuclideanDistance(x1, y1, x2, y2);
    }

    // add closing line
    if(isPoly){
      [x1, y1, x2, y2] = [coords[l-2], coords[l-1], coords[0], coords[1]]
      len += getEuclideanDistance(x1, y1, x2, y2)
    }
    return len
}
</code></pre>
<p>The only difference between these shapes is that <code>&lt;polygon&gt;</code> is self closing so we need to add the distance between 1st and last point.<br />
BTW: you can also retrieve all points as a clean point object array via native <code>poly.points</code> property (only in browsers).</p>
<h4><code>&lt;circle&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;ellipse&gt;</code></h4>
<p>As mentioned we have a perfect solution for circles but not for ellipses so we need an approximation. See also this great summary from <a href="https://www.mathsisfun.com/geometry/ellipse-perimeter.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">mathsisfun.com</a></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function getEllipsePerimeter(rx=0, ry=0) {
    // is circle – perfect solution
    if (rx === ry) {
        return 2 * Math.PI * rx;
    }

    let c=rx+ry
    let d = (rx - ry) / c;
    let h = d*d
    let totalLength = Math.PI * c  * (1 + 3 * h / (10 + Math.sqrt(4 - 3 * h) ));
    return totalLength;
}
</code></pre>
<h4><code>&lt;rect&gt;</code></h4>
<p>The main bummer can be the border rounding. If none is present we can simply use <code>height*2 + width*2</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// has corner rounding
if(atts.rx || atts.ry){
rx=atts.rx ? atts.rx : atts.ry
ry=atts.ry ? atts.ry : atts.rx

// normalize too large corner radii
if(rx&gt;width*0.5) rx=width*0.5;
if(ry&gt;height*0.5) ry=height*0.5;

width -=rx*2
height -=ry*2
ellipsePerimeter = getEllipsePerimeter(rx, ry)
}
len = width*2 + height*2 + ellipsePerimeter;
</code></pre>
<h2>Problem 3: Accuracy is relative</h2>
<p>Not all shapes provide a perfect closed form solution.
For instance we need to approximate the perimeter of an <code>&lt;ellipse&gt;</code>.<br />
In the above example I'm using the Ramanujan approximation for the sake of brevity. Fortunately, we don't need a scientific precision for presentation purposes. BTW. this also applies to cubic Béziers used in <code>&lt;path&gt;</code>.</p>
<p>Worth noting: using <code>getTotalLength()</code> returns other results in different browsers. Chromium is to this date particularly sloppy when it comes to circles.</p>
<h3><code>shape.pathLength</code> property?</h3>
<p>The <code>pathLength</code> property refers to the attribute of this name which is designed to set a custom calculation base for <code>dash-array</code> and <code> dash-offset</code> dash rendering behavior. It does not change or retrieve the actual length of an element. See <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/SVG/Reference/Attribute/pathLength" rel="nofollow noreferrer">&quot;mdn:pathLength&quot;</a></p>
<p>As you can see we can easily run into troubles when calculating lengths manually.</p>

Q: How do I share mouse events between multiple canvases? A: <p>Yes, you can &quot;share&quot; a event between multiple elements by <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/EventTarget/dispatchEvent" rel="nofollow noreferrer">dispatching</a> it manualy.</p>
<p>To be specific, you can capture the native mouse event in a separate element and re-dispatch it to your target canvas elements.</p>
<p>However, the native mouse event cannot be re-dispatched directly (A <code>InvalidStateError: The event is already being dispatched.</code> will be thrown if you try to dispatch the event to another element), so you need to make a copy of the original event via <code>new PointerEvent()</code>.</p>
<p>The new virtual event shares the same coordinate data with the native event, which shall satisfy the requirement in most cases. Then you can call the <code>dispatchEvent</code> function to dispatch it event to the target elements. (see demo below)</p>
<p>There are also a few things to note:</p>
<ul>
<li>If the native event handler is set on any ancestor of your targets, you also need to filter out any bubbled virtual event before the dispatching action. Otherwise the event will be triggered indefinitely.</li>
<li>Calling <code>preventDefault()</code> at the virtual event will take no direct effect to the native mouse event. You need to handle it manully within the dispatcher function.</li>
</ul>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function onMouseEvent(e){
  console.log(e.type, e.currentTarget.id, "at", e.clientX, e.clientY);
}
function onMouseEventPrevent(e){
  e.preventDefault();
  console.log(e.type, e.currentTarget.id, "at", e.clientX, e.clientY);
}
function eventDispatcher(e){
  // virtual events will bubble to the wrapper after dispatching
  // it's important to filter them out
  if (e._isvirtual) return; 

  let event = new PointerEvent(e.type, e); // make a virtual event
  event._isvirtual = true;
  for (const canvas of [canvas1, canvas2]){
    canvas.dispatchEvent(event)
    if (event.defaultPrevented) e.preventDefault();
    // if (canvas.dispatchEvent(event)===false) e.preventDefault(); // a simpler way
  }
}
canvas1.addEventListener('click', onMouseEvent);
canvas2.addEventListener('click', onMouseEvent);
canvas1.addEventListener('contextmenu', onMouseEvent);
canvas2.addEventListener('contextmenu', onMouseEventPrevent);
container.addEventListener('click', eventDispatcher);
container.addEventListener('contextmenu', eventDispatcher);</code></pre>
